---
import { Code } from "astro:components";
import GuideLayout from "../../components/GuideLayout.astro";
import { watershedCodeTheme } from "../../styles/code-theme";
import { excerpt, section } from "../../lib/excerpt";
import noteSource from "../../../../examples/retro_tutorial_lustre/src/note.gleam?raw";
import boardSource from "../../../../examples/retro_tutorial_lustre/src/board.gleam?raw";
import boardOpsSource from "../../../../examples/retro_tutorial_lustre/src/board_ops.gleam?raw";
import appSource from "../../../../examples/retro_tutorial_lustre/src/retro_tutorial_lustre.gleam?raw";

const noteRecord = excerpt(noteSource, "pub type Note {", "pub fn id(");
const codec = excerpt(
  noteSource,
  "pub fn to_json(",
  "pub fn from_register(",
);
const addNote = excerpt(boardOpsSource, "pub fn add_note(");
const readNotes = excerpt(boardOpsSource, "fn note_entries(");
const ordering = excerpt(
  boardSource,
  "fn notes_in_column(",
  "fn by_created_then_id(",
);
const unfiled = excerpt(boardSource, "fn unfiled(");
const addClicked = section(
  appSource,
  "    // These watershed mutations apply synchronously.",
  "    UpvoteClicked(id) ->",
);
---

<GuideLayout
  slug="notes"
  description="Step three of the watershed build guide: give every sticky note its own id in a RegisterMode OR-map, encode the whole note as one JSON register, and read it back through a decoder that keeps a malformed payload visible instead of crashing the board."
>
  <div class="g-block">
    <h2>Give the tie somewhere to go</h2>
    <p>
      Step 01’s first race had two people adding a note to <em>Went well</em> at
      the same instant. The reason a naive board loses one of them is not a bad
      merge rule — it’s a bad key. One key per column means one slot, and one
      slot can only hold one thing.
    </p>
    <p>
      So the notes channel is keyed by <strong>note</strong>, not by column, in
      an <a href="/structures/maps#ormap">OR-map</a> running in
      <code>RegisterMode</code>. Two concurrent adds write two different keys.
      There is no conflict left to resolve, and both notes survive because
      nothing ever asked them to compete.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="g-block">
    <h2>The whole note is one register</h2>
    <p>
      A <code>RegisterMode</code> leaf holds a string. That is the constraint the
      mode gives you, and the tutorial takes it literally: a note is encoded to
      JSON on the way in and decoded on the way out. Text, column, author, and a
      creation stamp travel together as one value.
    </p>
    <p>
      Which means the column lives <em>inside</em> the note. That is the trade:
      moving a note between columns is one whole-note write, so two people
      moving the same note at once resolve last-write-wins on that note. The
      tutorial doesn’t offer moves at all, and this is why. The{" "}
      <a href="/examples#retro_board_lustre">advanced retro board</a> splits the
      column into its own authoritative channel to get honest moves back.
    </p>
    <span class="g-file">src/note.gleam</span>
    <figure class="g-code">
      <Code code={noteRecord} lang="gleam" theme={watershedCodeTheme} />
    </figure>
    <figure class="g-code">
      <Code code={codec} lang="gleam" theme={watershedCodeTheme} />
    </figure>
    <div class="g-note">
      <p>
        <strong>A peer can write anything.</strong> The typed layer is a decode
        boundary, not a schema the server enforces — an older build, or a stale
        summary, can put any string in that register. So
        <code>from_register</code> has no error arm to forget: a payload that
        doesn’t decode becomes a visible placeholder card. A card you can see
        beats an exception that takes the whole board down.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="g-block">
    <h2>Adding a note is one map write</h2>
    <p>
      With the id scheme and the codec in place, adding a note is unremarkable,
      which is the point. Mint an id, encode, set. The nonce is there because two
      notes from one author can land in the same millisecond.
    </p>
    <span class="g-file">src/board_ops.gleam</span>
    <figure class="g-code">
      <Code code={addNote} lang="gleam" theme={watershedCodeTheme} />
    </figure>
    <p>
      <code>board_ops</code> exists so the UI and the tests call the identical
      write. A test that exercises its own copy of the mutation proves the copy
      works. Step 06 leans on this hard.
    </p>
    <span class="g-file">src/retro_tutorial_lustre.gleam</span>
    <figure class="g-code">
      <Code code={addClicked} lang="gleam" theme={watershedCodeTheme} />
    </figure>
  </div>

  <div class="g-block">
    <h2>Reading back: entries in, board out</h2>
    <p>
      <code>or_map_entries</code> hands you every live key with its leaf. In a
      register map every leaf is a <code>Register</code>, and the
      <code>let assert</code> below says so out loud: a <code>Tally</code> here
      would mean the channel was created in the wrong mode, which is a setup bug
      in your own bootstrap, not user data to route around.
    </p>
    <span class="g-file">src/board_ops.gleam</span>
    <figure class="g-code">
      <Code code={readNotes} lang="gleam" theme={watershedCodeTheme} />
    </figure>
    <p>
      From there the board is a pure function. Group by the column register,
      sort by <code>(created, id)</code>, and every replica renders the same
      order — including the two tied notes from step 01, where the created stamps
      match and the id breaks the tie identically everywhere.
    </p>
    <span class="g-file">src/board.gleam</span>
    <figure class="g-code">
      <Code code={ordering} lang="gleam" theme={watershedCodeTheme} />
    </figure>
    <div class="g-note">
      <p>
        <strong>Sort by content, never by arrival.</strong> Two replicas receive
        the same operations in the same sequenced order, but their local lists
        are not a rendering contract. Deriving the order from the note’s own
        fields is what makes “both clients show the same board” a thing you can
        assert.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="g-block">
    <h3>Deepening: the column you don’t recognise</h3>
    <p>
      Three columns are hardcoded, so what happens when a note arrives filed
      under <code>parking_lot</code> — a column a newer build added, or a
      register that came back as the placeholder? Dropping it would be the worst
      option available: the note is real, someone typed it, and it would vanish
      with no trace.
    </p>
    <figure class="g-code">
      <Code code={unfiled} lang="gleam" theme={watershedCodeTheme} />
    </figure>
    <p>
      Unknown columns land in <code>unfiled</code> and render in their own
      section. It costs one small function and it is the difference between a
      forward compatible board and a lossy one.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="g-out">
    <span class="annot">You can now</span>
    <p>
      Open the board in two tabs, add notes from both in the same second, and
      watch every tab settle on the same list in the same order — with a vote
      count of <code>0</code> under each one, because nothing can move it yet.
      Step 04 makes those numbers real.
    </p>
  </div>
</GuideLayout>
